{"question": "A 47-year-old man presents to his family physician with a sudden onset of severe pain and redness in his eyes that started this morning. He is having difficulty seeing properly and is extremely worried about losing his vision. Further history reveals that he has had progressive lower back pain for over 2 months now. The pain is usually at its worst in the morning, but it remains throughout the day. It gets better with movement, however, so he tends to do some light exercises every day. He also has heel pain and feels significant pressure while walking. Laboratory analysis reveals increased ESR and CRP serum levels. Which of the following would most likely be seen in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Dry mouth", "B": "Conjunctivitis", "C": "Malabsorption", "D": "Aortic regurgitation", "E": "Hemochromatosis"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Ophthalmology", "Rheumatology", "Internal Medicine", "Orthopedics", "Clinical Laboratory Science"], "option_domains": ["Ophthalmology", "Rheumatology"], "question_analyses": {"Ophthalmology": "The patient's symptoms suggest a systemic inflammatory condition that is affecting multiple parts of his body, including his eyes, back, and heels. The sudden onset of severe pain and redness in the eyes, along with difficulty seeing properly, is indicative of a condition known as anterior uveitis, which is inflammation of the middle layer of the eye, including the iris and ciliary body. This can be associated with systemic inflammatory conditions. The progressive lower back pain that has been ongoing for over two months, which is worse in the morning and improves with movement, is characteristic of an inflammatory back condition, possibly ankylosing spondylitis (AS). The heel pain and pressure while walking could be due to enthesitis, which is inflammation at the sites where tendons or ligaments insert into bone, commonly seen in conditions like AS. The laboratory findings of increased ESR (Erythr