{"question": "A researcher is conducting an experiment on the mouse kidney to study the relative concentrations between the tubular fluid and plasma of a number of substances along the proximal convoluted tubule. Based on the graph shown in figure A, which of the following best describes the tubular fluid-to-plasma concentration ratio of urea?", "options": {"A": "A", "B": "B", "C": "C", "D": "D", "E": "E"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Physiology", "Nephrology", "Experimental Medicine", "Biochemistry", "Anatomy"], "option_domains": ["Physiology", "Nephrology"], "question_analyses": {"Physiology": "In the context of the experiment described, the researcher is studying the relative concentrations of various substances between the tubular fluid and plasma along the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of a mouse kidney. The proximal convoluted tubule is a critical segment of the nephron where significant reabsorption of water and solutes occurs. The key aspect to focus on is the tubular fluid-to-plasma concentration ratio of urea. Urea is a waste product that is filtered out of the blood by the glomerulus and enters the tubular fluid. The handling of urea by the proximal convoluted tubule is particularly interesting because the PCT does not actively reabsorb urea; instead, it is subject to passive diffusion and back-diffusion processes. Given that the proximal convoluted tubule does not have a specific mechanism for urea reabsorption, the concentration of urea in the tubular fluid can vary depending on the osmotic gradient and the permeability of the tubule to urea. Typically, as the tubular fluid moves along the PCT, the concentration of urea may either increase or decrease, depending on the osmotic conditions and the rate of water reabsorption..", "Nephrology": "The scenario described involves a researcher studying the relative concentrations of various substances between the tubular fluid and plasma along the proximal convoluted tubule (